# Shared bnd include for any -test fragment that runs jGiven scenarios in-container.
# A host fragment writes just two lines of its own — its Fragment-Host, and a reactor-relative
# -include to THIS file (bnd's ${.} expands to the dir of the including bnd file; a true cross-jar
# classpath include is a bndtools-workspace feature, absent in plain bnd-maven-plugin):
#
#     Fragment-Host: <the-host-bundle-symbolic-name>
#     -include: ${.}/<relative-path>/jgiven-testkit/src/main/resources/jgiven-fragment.bnd
#
# and inherits everything below.
#
# bnd computes Import-Package from the scenario BYTECODE only. TWO jGiven packages the in-container
# run needs are invisible to that static analysis and must be forced, both because byte-buddy
# GENERATES classes into them at runtime (names absent from any bytecode, so bnd's
# com.tngtech.jgiven.* wildcard — which only emits imports for REFERENCED packages — drops them):
#   - com.tngtech.jgiven.impl.intercept — the stage proxy implements StageInterceptorInternal from it
#   - com.tngtech.jgiven.impl — byte-buddy defines ByteBuddyStageClassCreator$StepInterceptorGetter
#     Setter into this package when proxying a state-carrying Stage (@ProvidedScenarioState etc.); a
#     trivial Stage never triggers it, a real one does. The host must import the package so byte-buddy
#     can DEFINE the class into the host loader there.
# byte-buddy's own net.bytebuddy.* packages bnd DOES compute (statically reachable), so they need no
# forcing. These two forced imports are the entire build-time virality measure — bounded, named,
# contributed by the fragment to the host, single point of change for every jGiven host.
Import-Package: \
    !org.jspecify.annotations, \
    com.tngtech.jgiven.impl, \
    com.tngtech.jgiven.impl.intercept, \
    com.tngtech.jgiven.*, \
    *

-noimportjava: true
